{"Docs":[{"DocType":1,"IsAmended":false,"IsUpdated":false,"CompareStatus":4,"DocOrderId":221,"AmendedBy":[],"Notes":[],"Drafts":[],"ChunkGroupStartingNodeId":"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR","NodeDepth":2,"TitleHtml":"<div class=\"chunk-title\">Sec. 1-2. - Definitions and rules of construction.</div>","ShouldShowMiniToc":false,"Id":"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR_S1-2DERUCO","Title":"Sec. 1-2. - Definitions and rules of construction.","Content":"<div class=\"chunk-content\">\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               In the construction of this Code and of all ordinances and resolutions, the following\n               definitions and rules of construction shall be observed, unless inconsistent with\n               the manifest intent of the council or the context clearly requires otherwise. This\n               section is subject to <a href=\"\" data-chunk-id=\"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR_S1-10DEPODU\" class=\"section-link\" data-product-id=\"19967\">section 1-10</a>.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Charter.</span> The term \"Charter\" means the Charter of the City of Durham, North Carolina.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">City, municipality.</span> The terms \"city\" and \"municipality\" mean the City of Durham, in the State of North\n               Carolina. Where the context admits, it shall mean the city manager.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Code.</span> The terms \"the Code\" and \"this Code\" mean the Code of Ordinances, City of Durham,\n               North Carolina, as designated in <a href=\"\" data-chunk-id=\"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR_S1-1HOCODECI\" class=\"section-link\" data-product-id=\"19967\">section 1-1</a>.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Computation of time.</span> The time within which an act is to be done shall be computed by excluding the day\n               of the act, event, default or publication after which the designated period of time\n               begins to run and including the last day; unless the last day is a Saturday, Sunday\n               or legal holiday, in which event the period runs until the end of the next day which\n               is not a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday. When the period of time prescribed or\n               allowed is less than seven days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays\n               shall be excluded in the computation. A half holiday shall be considered as other\n               days and not as a legal holiday.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Conjunctions.</span> Conjunctions, such as \"and\" and \"or,\" shall each be deemed to include the other as\n               the context shall admit.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Council, governing body.</span> The terms \"council,\" \"city council,\" and \"governing body\" mean the city council of\n               the City of Durham.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">County.</span> The term \"county\" means the County of Durham, in the State of North Carolina. Where\n               the pertinent location is not in the County of Durham, the applicable county is intended.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Day, business day, working day.</span> The term \"day\" means a calendar day. The terms \"business day\" and \"working day\" are\n               equivalent to each other and exclude weekends and legal holidays. This definition\n               is subject to the definition of \"computation of time\" above.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Examples.</span> The principle that the expression of one thing is the exclusion of another will not\n               alone generally be sufficient to override the presumption that the provisions of this\n               chapter are to be used to construe all ordinances. For instance, if an ordinance twice\n               uses the word \"includes,\" and at one of those occasions it modifies that use with\n               the phrase \"but is not limited to,\" the presumption will still stand that the definition\n               of \"include\" in this <a href=\"\" data-chunk-id=\"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR_S1-2DERUCO\" class=\"section-link\" data-product-id=\"19967\">section 1-2</a> applies to the other use of \"includes\" in that ordinance.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">G.S., General Statutes.</span> The abbreviation \"G.S.\" and the expression \"General Statutes,\" mean the General Statutes\n               of North Carolina.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Gender.</span> Words importing one gender include the other genders.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Governmental person</span>. The term \"governmental person\" means government and an agency, subdivision, or unit\n               of government, such as a city, town, county, board of education, sanitary district,\n               airport authority, state, and the federal government. Depending on the context, the\n               term may include the City of Durham.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Holiday.</span> A \"holiday\" is a day observed by city government as a holiday or legal holiday as\n               provided in <a href=\"\" data-chunk-id=\"PTIICOOR_CH42PE_S42-16CIHO\" class=\"section-link\" data-product-id=\"19967\">section 42-16</a>(a), (b).\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Include.</span> The term \"include\" and its various forms are used without limitation.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Joint authority.</span> All words giving a joint authority to three or more persons or officers shall be\n               construed as giving such authority to a majority of such persons or officers.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Law, statute, rule, regulation, code.</span>\n               \n               <p class=\"incr1\">\n                  (a)\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               <p class=\"content2\">\n                  Where an ordinance or resolution incorporates by reference a law, statute, rule, regulation,\n                  standard, or code adopted by any person other than the city council, the law, statute,\n                  rule, regulation, standard, or code in effect on the date of adoption by the city\n                  council of the ordinance or resolution is incorporated.\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               \n               <p class=\"incr1\">\n                  (b)\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               <p class=\"content2\">\n                  Where the City Code as adopted in the 2008 recodification incorporates by reference\n                  a law, statute, rule, regulation, standard, or code adopted by any person other than\n                  the city council, subsection (a) of this definition shall not apply. Instead, the\n                  law, statute, rule, regulation, standard, or code in question is incorporated as it\n                  was in effect of the date of the adoption of the ordinance adopting the 2008 recodification.\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               \n               <p class=\"incr1\">\n                  (c)\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               <p class=\"content2\">\n                  References in ordinances and resolutions that are intended to call attention to laws,\n                  statutes, rules, regulations, standards, and codes, but which are not intended to\n                  incorporate by reference, are not subject to subsections (a) and (b) of this definition.\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               \n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Legal holiday.</span> A \"legal holiday\" is a day observed by city government as a holiday or legal holiday\n               as provided in <a href=\"\" data-chunk-id=\"PTIICOOR_CH42PE_S42-16CIHO\" class=\"section-link\" data-product-id=\"19967\">section 42-16</a>(a), (b).\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Manager, city manager.</span> The term \"manager\" or \"city manager\" means the city manager of the City of Durham\n               and his or her designee.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Month.</span> The term \"month\" means a calendar month.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Number.</span> Words used in the singular include the plural and the plural includes the singular\n               number.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Oath.</span> The term \"oath\" includes an affirmation in all cases in which, by law, an affirmation\n               may be substituted for an oath, and in such cases the terms \"swear\" and \"sworn\" are\n               equivalent to the terms \"affirm\" and \"affirmed.\"\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Officer.</span> Reference to an officer by title includes any duly authorized deputy or assistant\n               of such officer, or, to the extent allowed by law, the agent of such officer.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Officer, department, board, etc.</span> Reference to an officer, department, board or other entity by title refers to the\n               officer, department, board, etc., of the city in contrast with an officer, department,\n               board, etc., of any other entity.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Ordinance.</span> The term \"ordinance\" includes ordinances contained in this Code and ordinances omitted\n               from this Code.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Owner.</span> The term \"owner,\" applied to a building or land, includes any part owner, joint owner,\n               tenant in common, joint tenant or tenant by the entirety, of the whole or a part of\n               such building or land.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Person:</span>\n               \n               <p class=\"incr1\">\n                  (1)\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               <p class=\"content2\">\n                  The term \"person\" includes an individual, corporation, limited liability company,\n                  firm, partnership, association, organization, any group acting as a unit, and governmental\n                  person. The use of the term \"person\" in the ordinances and in resolutions, where either\n                  is adopted after the date of the adoption of the 2007 recodification of the City Code,\n                  is as stated in this subsection (1).\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               \n               <p class=\"incr1\">\n                  (2)\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               <p class=\"content2\">\n                  The use of the term \"person\" in ordinances and in resolutions, where either was adopted\n                  on or before the date of the adoption of the 2007 recodification of the City Code,\n                  shall be construed without consideration of subsection (1) of this definition.\n                  </p>\n               \t\t\n               \n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Personal property.</span> The term \"personal property\" includes every species of property except real property.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Preceding, following.</span> The terms \"preceding\" and \"following\" mean next before and next after, respectively.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Property.</span> The term \"property\" includes real and personal property.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Real property.</span> The term \"real property\" includes lands, tenements and hereditaments.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Registered mail.</span> The term \"registered mail\" includes certified mail.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Religious facility.</span> The expression \"religious facility\" means a building or structure, and any appurtenance\n               thereof, where the building or structure is primarily used or designated by a non-profit\n               organization for religious observance, services, worship, or instruction.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Shall, may.</span> The term \"shall\" is mandatory; the term \"may\" is permissive.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Sidewalk.</span> The term \"sidewalk\" means any portion of a street between the curbline and the adjacent\n               property line intended for the use of pedestrians.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Signature.</span> The term \"signature\" includes a mark when the person cannot write.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">State.</span> The term \"state\" means the State of North Carolina.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Street.</span> The term \"street\" includes any public way, road, highway, street, avenue, boulevard,\n               parkway, alley, lane, viaduct, and bridge and the approaches thereto, which has been\n               accepted for maintenance by the state or by the city.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Tenant, occupant.</span> The terms \"tenant\" and \"occupant,\" applied to a building or land, include any person\n               who leases or occupies, respectively, the whole or a part of such building or land,\n               whether alone or with others.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Time.</span> Words used in the past, present or future tense each include the other tenses.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">UDO, Unified Development Ordinance.</span> The term \"UDO\" means the Unified Development Ordinance. Unless the context indicates\n               otherwise, UDO and Unified Development Ordinance refer to the Unified Development\n               Ordinance of the city, rather than of the county.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Weekdays, weekend.</span> \"Weekdays\" are Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday. The \"weekend\" is\n               Saturday and Sunday.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Writing.</span> The terms \"writing\" and \"written\" includes printing and any other visual mode of\n               representing words and letters.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"p0\">\n               <span class=\"ital\">Year.</span> The term \"year\" means a calendar year in contrast to a fiscal or other year. However,\n               in some contexts the term \"year\" means the period of time between two dates such that\n               they are one year apart. For instance, \"during the two years before January 15, 2006\"\n               will generally mean January 15, 2004 through January 14, 2006 instead of January 1,\n               2004 through December 31, 2005.\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"historynote0\">\n               (Code 1982, § 1-2; Ord. No. 13574, Pt. 1, 4-7-2008)\n               </p>\n            <p class=\"refstatelaw0\" block_type=\"hang\"><b>State Law reference— </b>\n               Similar definitions and rules of statutory construction, G.S. 12-3, 160A-1; computation\n               of time, G.S. 1-593 (Rules of Civ. Proc., Rule 6(a)).\n               </p></div>","SortDate":null,"Footnotes":null}],"NewOrds":[],"PdfUrl":null,"LastOrd":null,"PrevNode":{"Id":"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR_S1-1HOCODECI","Heading":"Sec. 1-1. - How Code designated and cited.","NodeDepth":2,"HasChildren":false,"ParentId":"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR","DocOrderId":220,"Children":[],"Data":{"NodeKey":null,"IsUpdated":false,"IsAmended":false,"HasAmendedDescendant":false,"CompareStatus":4,"DocType":1,"DepthOverride":null,"ChunkGroupStartingId":null}},"NextNode":{"Id":"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR_S1-3SUCOORLIINTRNASEFE","Heading":"Sec. 1-3. - Supplementation of Code; ordinances of limited interest or transitory nature; setting fees.","NodeDepth":2,"HasChildren":false,"ParentId":"PTIICOOR_CH1GEPR","DocOrderId":222,"Children":[],"Data":{"NodeKey":null,"IsUpdated":false,"IsAmended":false,"HasAmendedDescendant":false,"CompareStatus":4,"DocType":1,"DepthOverride":null,"ChunkGroupStartingId":null}},"ShowToc":false}